# Go coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to modern Go
(1.23-1.25) idiom, following Effective Go and the Go Code Review Comments.
Applies only to `*.go` files.

## Naming and structure

- Package names are short, lowercase, no underscores; do not stutter the
  package name in exported identifiers (`user.User`, not `user.UserAccount`).
- Keep `main` thin: logic lives in packages under `internal/`,
  `cmd/<binary>/main.go` only wires flags/config.

## Interfaces and structs

- Accept interfaces, return structs: parameters take the narrowest interface
  the callee needs; a constructor returns the concrete `*Thing`.
- Define an interface at the consumer, one or two methods; a producer-side
  interface with one implementation is unnecessary.
- Prefer a zero-value-useful struct over requiring `New()` when the zero
  value is meaningful.

## Errors

- Every returned `error` is checked or visibly discarded
  (`_ = f.Close() // best-effort`).
- Wrap with `fmt.Errorf("doing x: %w", err)`, not `%v`; wrap once per
  meaningful layer, not at every call site.
- Compare with `errors.Is`/`errors.As`, never `err.Error() == "..."` or bare
  `==` against a wrapped error.
- Sentinel errors are `var ErrNotFound = errors.New("...")`, named
  `Err<Reason>`; a custom error type that wraps another implements
  `Unwrap() error`.

## Concurrency and context

- `context.Context` is the first parameter, named `ctx`, never stored on a
  struct field.
- Every goroutine has an owner that knows when it exits: `sync.WaitGroup`
  (or `WaitGroup.Go`), `errgroup.Group`, or the `ctx` it was given.
- Respect `ctx.Done()`/`ctx.Err()` in any loop or blocking call that should
  stop on cancel.

## Generics and modern idiom

- A type parameter only when it removes real duplication; do not generify a
  function with one concrete call site.
- `log/slog` for structured logging in new code, unless the project has
  standardized on something else.
- A `range-over-func` iterator only for a package's own lazy sequence API on
  Go 1.23+, not where a slice-returning function reads more clearly.

## Formatting and imports

- Format with `gofmt`/`goimports`; do not hand-format around a configured
  formatter.
- Imports grouped stdlib / third-party / local.
